Verified adversarially on candidate sha256:26d4ad92…f5e1bf (clone of freeze commit 96aaeb2, source fb9c221), under the concurrent three-reviewer load (load average 13-24 on 4 CPUs).

(1) The symptom is gone. The full module walk ('every import respects dependsOn, public surfaces and allowed packages') passed in every run:
- Node 24 full pnpm test: 28.4 s;
- Node 22 full run 1: 43.75 s, which would have timed out under the old shared 30 s budget, and passes under its own 150 s budget;
- Node 22 run 2: 23.7 s; Node 22 run 3: 22.6 s;
- alone at load ~13: 10.9 s (Node 22) and 10.5 s (Node 24), against 17.4 s alone in round 3, which is consistent with the claimed speed-up.
Node 22 runs 2 and 3 and the Node 24 run exit 0 with 2341/1711/259+2 skipped.

Node 22 run 1 exits 1. Its only failure is one vitest 'Unhandled Error: [vitest-worker]: Timeout calling "onTaskUpdate"' (vitest's own birpc 60 s timeout), with all 2341/1711/259+2 tests passing. I tested whether the boundary walk's long synchronous stretch could cause it. A disposable test that blocks a worker synchronously for 70 s (and a 45 s control) does NOT produce the error: exit 0, 3/3 (repro/zz-sec-r4-sync-block.log). So it is not a residual of this finding. It is the main vitest process overloaded at load ~17 (that run reports collect 574 s, prepare 52.9 s), the same RPC error round 3 saw once, and it did not recur in runs 2 and 3.

(2) EMPTY_EMIT cannot hide a syntax error the old path caught.
- Source reading (TypeScript 6.0.2, lib/typescript.js): transpileWorker adds program.getSyntacticDiagnostics(sourceFile) and getOptionsDiagnostics() BEFORE program.emit; a custom 'before' transformer runs only inside emitJsFileOrBundle -> transformNodes. Every context.addDiagnostic call in the compiler is inside transformDeclarations (lines 119080-120790), which transpileModule turns off (declaration=false). On the script path emitFiles collects no transform diagnostics: it collects only declarationTransform.diagnostics and writeFile failures. noEmitOnError is unset, so handleNoEmitOptions adds nothing.
- My own hostile differential (repro/zz-sec-r4-rule4-differential.test.ts) compares the candidate's syntaxErrors() with the pre-repair call (the same transpileModule options without the transformer, i.e. bbd6294's body):
  - 101 hostile sources x 8 extensions = 808 cases, 290 of them with errors. The sources cover strict-mode, octal and escape errors, regex flags, using/await using, decorators, #private, JSX errors, import attributes and assertions, export =, shebang placement, unterminated comments, strings and templates, html comments, and more;
  - all 448 real module files, verbatim and with 3 seeded random mutations each: 1792 cases, 654 with errors;
  - 0 differences.

(3) The guard test has teeth. My own mutation run (repro/rule4-mutants.sh, .log) applies five regressions to syntaxErrors in the clone:
- M1 reportDiagnostics false: KILLED by the guard and by the P20 planted control;
- M2 fileName fixed to module.ts: KILLED by the guard and by the F-DG1-134 JSX control;
- M3 only the first error reported: KILLED, by the guard alone;
- M4 a parser-only createSourceFile path: KILLED, by the guard alone;
- M5 target ES2015: SURVIVED. This is the handback's disclosed equivalent mutant. My probe (repro/rule4-target-probe.log) shows that in TS 6.0.2 none of /a/v, named groups, /s, top-level await, #private, 1n or using gives a syntactic diagnostic that depends on the target between the default and ES2015 (only ES5 adds an options-deprecation diagnostic). So D-175 observation (o) is correct.
The clone's source was restored after each mutant (git status clean).

(4) No control changed. git diff bbd6294..fb9c221 of architecture.test.ts and architecture.testkit.ts removes exactly two lines:
- the full walk's 'AST_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS,' argument, now FULL_MODULE_WALK_TIMEOUT_MS;
- 'function syntaxErrors(', now exported for the guard.
Everything else is added. The orchestrator's 2d0f98c changes comment lines only (no non-comment line in its diff). All 140 architecture tests pass, including every planted-violation and allow-list self-check. AST_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS stays 30 s for the five module-owned tests.

Residual, not raised: under extreme load the Node 22 full suite can still exit 1 through vitest's own RPC timeout, as run 1 shows. That is a property of the shared test runner under overload, not of this test or its budget, as my 70 s block experiment shows.
